\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Pose tracking against ground truth, 12 November 2026. Route shapes were driven by hand: straight, approximately square, approximately a figure-of-eight. ``Over 1\,m'' is the longest unbroken stretch with the error above a metre and ``ended'' the median over the last tenth of the run, i.e. whether it came back. Offset and scatter, reported in the text, are \SIrange {0.07}{0.27}{\metre } and \SIrange {0.04}{0.24}{\metre } respectively. Blind is the share of localisation poses carrying RTAB-Map's no-information covariance. Ground truth is compared without a lever arm, for the reason given under Frames.\relax }}{3}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:exp4-tracking}{{4.1}{3}{Pose tracking against ground truth, 12 November 2026. Route shapes were driven by hand: straight, approximately square, approximately a figure-of-eight. ``Over 1\,m'' is the longest unbroken stretch with the error above a metre and ``ended'' the median over the last tenth of the run, i.e. whether it came back. Offset and scatter, reported in the text, are \SIrange {0.07}{0.27}{\metre } and \SIrange {0.04}{0.24}{\metre } respectively. Blind is the share of localisation poses carrying RTAB-Map's no-information covariance. Ground truth is compared without a lever arm, for the reason given under Frames.\relax }{table.caption.8}{}}
\newlabel{tab:exp4-tracking@cref}{{[table][1][4]4.1}{[1][2][]3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Every run on one axis: planar error against distance driven since the first fix, logarithmic. Distance rather than time, so that runs driven at different speeds compare. The two grey lines are the rms accuracy Experiment\nobreakspace  {}3 measures for these priors' own graphs, \SIrange {0.10}{0.40}{\metre }: most of the time most runs sit between them, which is the bound this slot reports. The two shelves are the excursions of \cref  {tab:exp4-tracking} --- flat, not ramped, so the pose steps to a wrong place and holds it rather than drifting away. Both end abruptly when a correct fix lands.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp4-summary}{{4.1}{3}{Every run on one axis: planar error against distance driven since the first fix, logarithmic. Distance rather than time, so that runs driven at different speeds compare. The two grey lines are the rms accuracy Experiment~3 measures for these priors' own graphs, \SIrange {0.10}{0.40}{\metre }: most of the time most runs sit between them, which is the bound this slot reports. The two shelves are the excursions of \cref {tab:exp4-tracking} --- flat, not ramped, so the pose steps to a wrong place and holds it rather than drifting away. Both end abruptly when a correct fix lands.\relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
\newlabel{fig:exp4-summary@cref}{{[figure][1][4]4.1}{[1][2][]3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces \texttt  {U\_static}, figure-of-eight, \SI {152}{\metre }. Left: the published pose against ground truth on the world's collision-mesh footprints, the same underlay the chapter 3 scene figures use. Right: the same two tracks on the prior's exported occupancy grid, brought into the world frame by the fit of \cref  {sec:exp4-sim}. The grid's shelf rows landing on the mesh footprints is the alignment confirming itself; the thick band at the right wall against the mesh's thin line is the wall displacement Experiment\nobreakspace  {}3 reports. The dashed segment is the pose before the first fix, which is the seed composed with raw odometry and is not a tracking error. Median error \SI {0.098}{\metre }.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp4-normal}{{4.2}{4}{\texttt {U\_static}, figure-of-eight, \SI {152}{\metre }. Left: the published pose against ground truth on the world's collision-mesh footprints, the same underlay the chapter 3 scene figures use. Right: the same two tracks on the prior's exported occupancy grid, brought into the world frame by the fit of \cref {sec:exp4-sim}. The grid's shelf rows landing on the mesh footprints is the alignment confirming itself; the thick band at the right wall against the mesh's thin line is the wall displacement Experiment~3 reports. The dashed segment is the pose before the first fix, which is the seed composed with raw odometry and is not a tracking error. Median error \SI {0.098}{\metre }.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:exp4-normal@cref}{{[figure][2][4]4.2}{[1][2][]4}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces \texttt  {I\_static}, figure-of-eight, \SI {124}{\metre }: the cleanest run of the seven, median \SI {0.136}{\metre } and maximum \SI {0.93}{\metre }, on the second layout and its own prior (\texttt  {dataset_allsensor_realistic_01_map}, 23\,255 occupied cells). The layouts differ in aisle arrangement, so this is not a repeat of \cref  {fig:exp4-normal} in a different place: the route crosses its own path twice and the pose holds through both crossings, which is where a prior that aliases between aisles would show it. Conventions as \cref  {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp4-ilayout}{{4.3}{4}{\texttt {I\_static}, figure-of-eight, \SI {124}{\metre }: the cleanest run of the seven, median \SI {0.136}{\metre } and maximum \SI {0.93}{\metre }, on the second layout and its own prior (\repo {dataset_allsensor_realistic_01_map}, 23\,255 occupied cells). The layouts differ in aisle arrangement, so this is not a repeat of \cref {fig:exp4-normal} in a different place: the route crosses its own path twice and the pose holds through both crossings, which is where a prior that aliases between aisles would show it. Conventions as \cref {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:exp4-ilayout@cref}{{[figure][3][4]4.3}{[1][2][]4}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces \texttt  {U\_dynamic}, straight, \SI {26}{\metre }: the worst run of the seven on both offset (\SI {0.274}{\metre }) and scatter (\SI {0.240}{\metre }), and the only one with traffic. The published pose runs beside ground truth down the aisle rather than diverging from it, which is what a displacement rather than a drift looks like. Conventions as \cref  {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp4-offset}{{4.4}{5}{\texttt {U\_dynamic}, straight, \SI {26}{\metre }: the worst run of the seven on both offset (\SI {0.274}{\metre }) and scatter (\SI {0.240}{\metre }), and the only one with traffic. The published pose runs beside ground truth down the aisle rather than diverging from it, which is what a displacement rather than a drift looks like. Conventions as \cref {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:exp4-offset@cref}{{[figure][4][4]4.4}{[1][2][]5}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces \texttt  {U\_static}, approximately square, \SI {109}{\metre }: the one run of the seven that lost the pose. From about $y=-5$ the published pose leaves the aisle and cuts diagonally through three shelf rows while ground truth follows the aisle, which is the \SI {6.09}{\metre } second-half median of \cref  {tab:exp4-tracking} seen directly. A pose that passes through shelving is wrong in a way no error statistic states as plainly. Conventions as \cref  {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp4-diverged}{{4.5}{5}{\texttt {U\_static}, approximately square, \SI {109}{\metre }: the one run of the seven that lost the pose. From about $y=-5$ the published pose leaves the aisle and cuts diagonally through three shelf rows while ground truth follows the aisle, which is the \SI {6.09}{\metre } second-half median of \cref {tab:exp4-tracking} seen directly. A pose that passes through shelving is wrong in a way no error statistic states as plainly. Conventions as \cref {fig:exp4-normal}.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:exp4-diverged@cref}{{[figure][5][4]4.5}{[1][2][]5}}
